## 1️⃣ Security‑Focused Improvements  

| Issue | Why it matters | Recommended change (code snippet) |
|-------|----------------|-----------------------------------|
| **Missing HTTP security headers** | Browsers can enforce protections (e.g., X‑Content‑Type‑Options, CSP, HSTS) only if you send the proper headers. | ```js\nconst helmet = require('helmet');\napp.use(helmet()); // add early, before routes\n``` |
| **No rate‑limiting** | Prevents brute‑force, DoS, and credential‑stuffing attacks. | ```js\nconst rateLimit = require('express-rate-limit');\nconst limiter = rateLimit({\n  windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000, // 15 min\n  max: 100, // limit each IP to 100 requests per window\n  standardHeaders: true,\n  legacyHeaders: false,\n});\napp.use(limiter);\n``` |
| **CORS is unrestricted** (default → allow all) | Exposes API to any origin, which may be undesirable. | ```js\nconst cors = require('cors');\napp.use(cors({\n  origin: process.env.ALLOWED_ORIGINS?.split(',') || [], // whitelist via env var\n  methods: ['GET','POST','PUT','DELETE'],\n}));\n``` |
| **Hard‑coded port** | Makes the app inflexible and can expose it on the wrong interface in production. | ```js\nconst PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;\napp.listen(PORT, () => console.log(`Server running on port ${PORT}`));\n``` |
| **Missing `trust proxy` for correct IP handling** (important when behind a reverse proxy/load‑balancer) | Needed for proper rate‑limit / logging / secure cookies. | ```js\napp.set('trust proxy', 1); // if behind one proxy\n``` |
| **No HTTPS enforcement** (if you eventually terminate TLS at the app) | Prevents downgrade attacks. | ```js\napp.use((req, res, next) => {\n  if (req.secure) return next();\n  res.redirect(`https://${req.headers.host}${req.url}`);\n});\n``` |
| **No security‑oriented logging** | You want to capture abnormal activity without leaking sensitive data. Consider using **winston** or **pino** with a redacted format. | ```js\nconst pino = require('pino');\nconst logger = pino({ level: process.env.LOG_LEVEL || 'info' });\napp.use((req, res, next) => {\n  logger.info({ method: req.method, url: req.originalUrl, ip: req.ip });\n  next();\n});\n``` |

---

## 2️⃣ Robust Error‑Handling  

| Issue | Why it matters | Recommended change (code snippet) |
|-------|----------------|-----------------------------------|
| **No global error handler** | Uncaught errors will crash the process or expose stack traces to the client. | ```js\n// place after all routes\napp.use((err, req, res, next) => {\n  console.error(err); // could be logger instead\n  const status = err.status || 500;\n  const message = status === 500 ? 'Internal Server Error' : err.message;\n  res.status(status).json({ error: message });\n});\n``` |
| **No handling for async route errors** | `async` functions that reject will bypass Express's default error flow. | Use a wrapper helper: <br>```js\nconst asyncHandler = fn => (req, res, next) => Promise.resolve(fn(req, res, next)).catch(next);\n\napp.get('/users', asyncHandler(async (req, res) => {\n  // future async DB call here\n  res.json([...]);\n}));\n``` |
| **Uncaught exceptions / rejections** | Process may terminate unexpectedly, causing downtime. | ```js\nprocess.on('uncaughtException', err => {\n  console.error('Uncaught Exception:', err);\n  // optional graceful shutdown\n});\nprocess.on('unhandledRejection', (reason, promise) => {\n  console.error('Unhandled Rejection at:', promise, 'reason:', reason);\n});\n``` |
| **Exposing raw stack traces** (development vs. production) | Attackers can learn internals. | In the error handler, only send the stack when `NODE_ENV !== 'production'`. |
| **Missing 404 handler** | Requests to unknown routes currently hang or return default HTML. | ```js\napp.use((req, res) => {\n  res.status(404).json({ error: 'Not Found' });\n});\n``` |

---

## 3️⃣ Input Validation & Data Sanitisation  

Even though the current routes are all **GET** and take no user‑provided data, it’s good practice to lay the groundwork for future endpoints.

| Recommendation | Library / Approach | Example |
|----------------|-------------------|---------|
| **Validate query parameters, URL params, and request bodies** | `express-validator`, `joi`, `zod`, or `yup` | ```js\nconst { query, validationResult } = require('express-validator');\napp.get('/search', [\n  query('q').trim().isLength({ min: 1, max: 100 }).escape()\n], (req, res, next) => {\n  const errors = validationResult(req);\n  if (!errors.isEmpty()) return res.status(400).json({ errors: errors.array() });\n  // safe to use req.query.q now\n});\n``` |
| **Sanitise data before storing / sending** | Use the same validator libraries or manual escaping for DB/HTML contexts. | `escape()` (as above) or a library like `xss-clean`. |
| **Content‑type enforcement** | Prevent JSON‑injection or unwanted bodies. | ```js\napp.use(express.json({ limit: '100kb', verify: (req, res, buf) => {\n  if (req.headers['content-type'] !== 'application/json') {\n    throw new Error('Only application/json allowed');\n  }\n}}));\n``` |
| **Disable `x-powered-by` header** (leaks framework version) | Express adds it by default. | ```js\napp.disable('x-powered-by');\n``` |

