Because the current endpoints are “GET‑only” and return static data, there isn’t any obvious input that needs validation *right now*. However, any real‑world extension (e.g., adding POST/PUT/DELETE routes, query parameters, authentication, etc.) will immediately inherit the following gaps:

| Gap | Why it matters (security) |
|-----|---------------------------|
| **No “helmet” / security headers** | Browsers won’t receive protections against XSS, click‑jacking, MIME sniffing, etc. |
| **No rate‑limiting** | Makes the service vulnerable to denial‑of‑service or credential‑brute‑force attacks once auth is added. |
| **No input sanitisation/validation** | If you later accept query strings, request bodies, or URL params, malicious payloads could be processed (e.g., NoSQL/SQL injection, prototype pollution). |
| **No centralized error handling** | Uncaught errors will crash the process or leak stack traces to the client. |
| **Hard‑coded port & console logging** | Makes configuration inflexible and can leak internal info in production logs. |
| **Missing CORS policy** | If this API is called from browsers, the default “allow all” policy may be undesirable. |
| **No HTTPS enforcement** | In production you’d typically sit behind a reverse proxy, but it’s good to reject clear‑text requests early. |
| **No request‑size limits** | Prevents oversized payload attacks (once you add body parsers). |

